#### Targeting and Checks

> **Scaling (op) Effects**
>
> The opportunities described on this page can generally scale for effect, becoming more efficacious if additional (op) symbols are spent on them. For uses of (op) whose specific effects are spelled out in text, scaling is indicated with a “+” symbol, and opportunities in text without the “+” symbol do not scale.
>
> If the cost of an opportunity is “(op)+,” the character may spend 1 or more (op) when activating the effect. The effect then scales; the more (op) that was spent to activate it, the more impactful it becomes.

> **EXAMPLE USES OF (op)**
>
> Beyond the narrative uses discussed here, an extensive list of example uses of (op) can be found in Table 8–1: Examples of (op) (see page 328). Players and GMs can use these directly, or as inspiration for their own new opportunities.

### Using Opportunity

#### (op) as a Descriptor

For instance, if a courtier fails a check to convince their lord to cancel a planned pilgrimage after an assassination attempt, spending Air (op) to have spoken very subtly might prevent their lord from realizing they were trying to exert influence at all. Alternatively, speaking very cautiously with Earth (op) might convince their lord that they worry too much, but their intentions are good—perhaps the lord will invite their loyal retainer along for the pilgrimage, so that they can see how safe it is and relax a bit. In both cases, the character still failed, but their opportunity added an interesting, advantageous twist.

Many of the example uses of (op) (see page 328) are thematically linked to these concepts, and players should feel free to use them for inspiration.

#### (op) as a Narrative Tool

> **Narrating Strife**
>
> Players should feel free to narrate the emotions their characters are feeling as strife accumulates, as well as how it may affect their characters’ mannerisms and behavior. Emotion—both bearing it stoically and expressing it openly—is an important aspect of drama. While samurai drama often revolves around stoic characters, stoicism is at its most compelling when characters are feeling something very deeply inside. Even giving small descriptions of their characters’ current emotional states can help players engage with this theme. Of course, not all strife needs to be narrated—it is up to the players to decide when strife feels impactful or important.
>
> Strife from checks can represent run-of-the-mill frustration, the joy of a challenge, old pa dredged up by the present situation, fear, shame, rage, passion, hope, the thrill of victory, or any number of other feelings that heighten the character’s emotional state.

> **NERVOUS HABITS AND TELLS**
>
> During character creation, a player will determine one or more tells that show the character is under stress. These are the sorts of small details that help a character feel vivid, and players can use them to flavor their descriptions when their characters accumulate strife or unmask.

