### The Character

Characters possess a number of major mechanical attributes and abilities that reflect their upbringing, natural affinities and weaknesses, accumulated wisdom, and training, which are described in the following pages.

#### The Five Rings

##### Air

The Air Ring represents a graceful, cunning, and precise personality. This approach is subtle or layered with nuance, as the character moves too nimbly or speaks too obliquely to be pinned down. A character with a high Air Ring value is eloquent, physically and socially deft, and shrewd. Depending on the task, characters can use an Air approach (approach names are called out in the subsequent section in bold) as follows:

##### Earth

The Earth Ring represents a character’s aptitude for taking a steady, thorough, and grounded approach to problems. This approach is cautious and considerate, as the character works to get results without suffering losses or taking unneeded risks. A character with a high Earth Ring value is likely to be physically tough, mentally resilient, and reliable. Depending on the task, characters can use an Earth approach (approach names are called out in the subsequent section in bold) as follows:

##### Fire

The Fire Ring represents a ferocious, direct, and inventive personality. This approach is explosive and intense, as the character works to get results no matter the cost to themself or others. A character with a high Fire Ring value is likely to be passionate, curious, physically quick, occasionally brusque, and a fast learner. Depending on the task, characters can use a Fire approach (approach names are called out in the subsequent section in bold) as follows:

##### Water

The Water Ring represents an adaptable, powerful, and perceptive personality. The approach is balanced and reversible, as the character works to achieve results without overextending themself or underperforming. A character with a high Water Ring value is naturally affable, physically flexible, observant, and easygoing. Depending on the task, characters can use a Water approach (approach names are called out in the subsequent section in bold) as follows:

##### Void

The Void Ring represents a centered, unflinching personality; it can also represent the “flow state.” By letting go of the suffering inherent in one’s ego-driven desires, the samurai can transcend earthly limitations. It is the enlightened approach, accepting the nature of all Elements, yet allowing no one Element to dominate. A character with a high Void Ring value is likely to be spiritually sensitive, wise, and introspective. Depending on the task, characters can use a Void approach (approach names are called out in the subsequent section in bold) as follows:

> **Miyamoto Musashi on Mastery**
>
> Legend of the Five Rings owes a great deal to the real world historical writings of Miyamoto Musashi, a famous Japanese sword master. The Book of Five Rings (c. 1645) is his martial and philosophical manual, and offers insights on fighting and how these apply to life more generally.
>
> Interestingly, Musashi did not write his account until decades after his legendary career as a duelist. Further, reflecting on his success in youth, he describes how he did not reach the underlying truth of martial arts until late in his life, decades after his last known duel. Despite achieving the height of his demonstrated fighting ability early in his career, he felt that his understanding of martial arts—and, by extension, all other arts he learned—was incomplete until he had spent another twenty years studying and reflecting.
>
> In this game, reaching Rank 5 in a skill is akin to Musashi reaching his peak as a practicing duelist in his youth. It is an incredible achievement, but does not yet reflect full understanding of the art or the wider implications of the idea of mastery itself. To achieve their greatest potential, a character must not only advance their skills and rings, but also come to understand themself and their place in the world.

> **“YOU” THE PLAYER AND “YOU” THE CHARACTER**
>
> Character abilities such as advantages and techniques are written in second person for clarity.
>
> As the player, you make all decisions for your character, who does what is described in the ability within the fiction of the game.

#### Skill Ranks

#### Advantages and Disadvantages

Advantages and disadvantages are intrinsic quirks of a character’s body, mind, personality, or spirit. A character is assigned several of these at character creation, but they can also acquire more during play as a result of narrative events or through certain circumstances (such as suffering particularly grievous Critical Strikes, as described on page 269). Each advantage and disadvantage is assigned to the specific ring to which it most often pertains. The most common categories of advantages and disadvantages are distinctions, passions, adversities, and anxieties, and a character is assigned at least one of each of these during the process of character creation.

For more details, see Advantages and Disadvantages, on page 99.

> **A HERO’S RESOLVE**
>
> In works of samurai fiction, force of will is often a deciding factor at crucial moments. Void points can be spent to tap into that spirit of heroism, helping characters persevere in situations that seem insurmountable.

#### Derived Attributes

Derived attributes are numerical values that are determined by a character’s ring values and other decisions made at character creation, and they can be altered over the course of play.

#### Spending Void Points

When a player spends a Void point for an effect, they are encouraged to offer a brief explanation about why their character chooses to draw a line at that moment and what parts of their character’s core beliefs and motivations factor into it.

Void points can be spent in a number of ways, as described below:

#### Gaining Void Points

Void points are gained through facing adversity, for without struggle, there can be no triumph. Characters can gain Void points in a number of ways, most of them representing moments in which the character faces adversity and is galvanized to succeed by it. A few common instances include:

> **FORFEITING AND STAKING ATTRIBUTES BELOW 0**
>
> A character whose attribute is lower than the amount they would need to forfeit can still do the deed in question. Characters with honor, glory, or status 0 can forfeit as much as they desire—the state of their faith in Bushidō, reputation, or social standing cannot sink any lower!
>
> However, a character cannot stake honor, glory, or status they do not have.

> **GM GUIDANCE ON SOCIAL ATTRIBUTES**
>
> Chapter 7: The Game Master has resources for GMs to use social attributes during the game.
>
> If a character’s honor or glory increases above 64 or falls below 30, they begin to receive advantages or disadvantages as described in these tables.

#### Honor, Glory, and Status

##### Value and Rank

##### Increasing Social Attributes

These three attributes can increase over the life of a character, usually via an honor, glory, or status award from the GM. See the section beginning on page 300 for recommendations on ways social attributes increase.

##### Decreasing Social Attributes

Any of these three attributes can also decrease over the life of a character. Rather than being arbitrated by the GM, a decrease to a social attribute always occurs because the character’s player chooses to forfeit or stake it to achieve some end the character desires—or because their player feels that it is good storytelling.

Decreasing any of these attributes should be a conscious choice on the part of the player, not a penalty imposed by the GM.

##### Forfeiting Social Attributes

##### Staking Social Attributes

##### Status

> **IMPOSSIBLE DESIRES**
>
> Some especially existential ninjō might not be resolvable at all, at least not within a single lifetime, and this is also acceptable as long as the character has a clear path to try to seek their unachievable goal.

#### Ninjō (Desire) and Giri (Duty)

##### Choosing Ninjō

##### Ninjō and Strife

##### Resolving Ninjō

> **NO LORD TO SERVE**
>
> If the character has no lord to serve due to events in the story, their giri could become finding a new lord to serve, or it could be fulfilling the final order of their lord.

> **SAMPLE GIRI**
>
> Restore the reputation of your teacher’s dōjō
>
> Invent a new weapon that will give your clan an advantage
>
> Protect your lord (or other charge who appears in the campaign)
>
> Capture a particular region of land held by an enemy clan
>
> Persuade a powerful leader to join your lord
>
> Negotiate a trade agreement with a major ally
>
> Gather information and use it to topple an enemy faction
>
> Keep a particular secret of your lord from becoming public
>
> Maintain the reputation or prosperity of a specific shrine
>
> Attend to the needs of a particular kami
>
> Retrieve a set of sacred artifacts for your clan
>
> Assassinate specific members of an enemy faction

