## The Game Master

## CHAPTER 7

> **CHAPTER PRESENTATION**
>
> The first part of this chapter focuses on the basics of being a game master for Legend of the Five Rings. It addresses matters such as the role of the GM, how to organize and manage a group of players, how to evoke the setting, and how to set up story scenarios.
>
> The rest of this chapter introduces GMs to their role and provides them with the tools they need to run a smooth, fun, and exciting game, along with extra options such as alternate campaign styles.

> **STARTING A NEW CAMPAIGN**
>
> The beginning of a new campaign is a good time for the GM to offer some background context: “The border dispute between the Lion and the Crane has been ramping up since the death of Akodo Arasou,” or “The creatures of the Shadowlands have been abnormally quiet this month…” It is best if these help foreshadow the conflict you’re about to introduce, and raise new questions in the players’ minds.

### The Role of the GM

#### Who’s in Charge Here?

#### How to start a campaign

#### How to run a session

##### Preparations

##### Getting Things Moving

> **HOW OFTEN TO PLAY**
>
> Once a week or once every other week is the standard rate of play, which gives you time to prepare adventures between sessions, as well as giving your group some flexibility to reschedule a session if other events conflict with it.

> **LEARNING THE ROPES**
>
> For a player brand new to the game system, it can be useful to let them play a character starting out of the gate to get a feel for the rules, and then catch them up in terms of experience points after a session or two. In such cases, the GM should adjust the TNs for their checks to help them succeed with the same frequency as the rest of the group.

##### Running a Scene

##### Ending a Session

#### Managing Players

##### Being Respectful of Each Other

> **Your Game, Your ROkugan**
>
> Different players come to the Legend of the Five Rings RPG for different reasons. Perhaps they have heard of or played the associated card games. Some may be drawn due to their love for samurai fiction and cinema, such as the Akira Kurosawa films and the books by Eiji Yoshikawa and James Clavell. Many L5R fans began as students of Japanese history or culture, as fans of anime and manga, or both.
>
> Others are general fans of science fiction and fantasy, from romantic stories with political intrigues to epic tales of good and evil or shades of moral gray. Often, the source material that the GM is most familiar with will color the types of stories and tone of the game, and that’s absolutely normal (and encouraged—run what you love!). Besides differences in dramatic taste, different groups will play the game differently. You may find yourself absorbed in the narrative and the relationships between characters. Other groups focus on fine-tuning their characters’ abilities and exploring the tactical side of combat. This game is designed to support numerous styles of play and, as GM, you are empowered to jettison, rework, or simplify rules as you desire. Throughout this chapter, there are several margin notes addressing how to tailor your game to a particular archetype of group (though you’ll need to find the right balance for your own unique group of players).

> **ABSENCES**
>
> Often, players need to skip a session, and groups should agree on what to do when this happens. One option is that the GM or another player can take temporary control of the character as an NPC. Another is the characters can be absent from adventures while they deal with some task off-screen.

##### Dealing with Disruptions

##### Arrivals and Departures

##### On “Metagaming”

> **ROOM TO BREATHE**
>
> When planning an adventure, you should be sure to include a variety of conflicts and challenges to evoke alternating emotions. If a story is all excitement and hope, the audience can begin to feel exhausted. If a story is all despair and fear, the audience may feel drained. Most stories switch back and forth between showing the protagonists’ successes and failures, to build toward even more powerful emotional moments.
>
> By including a variety of conflicts and challenges, the GM also ensures that all the characters get to contribute and share in the limelight, which keeps them engaged.

#### Evoking the Legend of the Five Rings Setting

##### Making Locations Memorable

##### Bringing NPCs to Life

> **Story-First Groups**
>
> For some groups, playing the game is secondary to exploring their characters’ motivations and fates, and the thrill of “what happens next?” trumps the thrill of using particular combinations of techniques to defeat enemies. If your group falls into this category, you might consider one or more of the following:
>
> - Techniques are reduced to purely narrative effects.
> - Combat-related conflicts are mediated with a single roll, such as using the rules in One-Roll Duels (see page 261).
> - Intrigues are handled purely as roleplay, with the GM determining who prevails based only on what players say (or describe their characters to be saying).

##### Dramatizing Bushidō

##### Navigating Status and Hierarchy

> **Roleplaying the Spirits**
>
> Excepting those from Tengoku and Jigoku, spirits don’t operate on a continuum of good and evil. They act according to their nature, which is governed by its own, inscrutable rules. These creatures have no consideration for the welfare or desires of the samurai they encounter. The exception to this is ancestor spirits, who are intrinsically invested in seeing their descendants succeed and act in ways that reflect well on their ancestors.
>
> Thus, you should strive to make an otherworldly being’s motives mysterious, as well as use doublespeak, riddles, or illusions to trick your PCs. The very same spirits who help the PCs on a lark may turn on the party for their own amusement later.
>
> Adding supernatural elements to investigations or exploratory scenes can be especially effective at making the characters approach obstacles with fresh eyes and a sense of wonder—and usually, by the end, a new wariness about dealing with spirits and monsters.

##### Approaching the Supernatural

> **Grittier Games**
>
> If your group wants an extremely lethal experience for their PCs, you could apply one or more of the following to make the game substantially more likely to result in death and dismemberment:
>
> - Increase the Deadliness of all weapons by +4.
> - If a character has the Bleeding condition at the end of the scene, they perish unless they spend a Void point.
> - The Lightly Wounded and Severely Wounded condition can fester. Each time a character fails a Fitness check to remove this condition, the conditions worsen (from Lightly Wounded to Severely Wounded, or Severely Wounded to dead).

#### Offering Choices and Determining Consequences

#### Creating Scenarios

##### Devising Goals

> **Tactical Groups**
>
> Tactically minded players tend to prioritize understanding and exploiting the rules of the game. They might not be as invested in the backstories of their own characters, much less those of non-player characters, and may see combat as its own end, because it’s a chance to test out a cool combination of techniques and abilities. If your group has some of these traits, consider the following alterations to the rules:
>
> - Encourage roleplaying, but make mechanics a major focus during conflicts like intrigues.
> - Make all rolls open, and tell the players the TN of all checks they make.
> - Instead of just describing their narrative effects, fully explain NPC abilities in mechanical terms, so your players can understand and take advantage of how these interact with their characters’ own techniques and abilities.

> **LINEAR AND FREEFORM SCENARIOS**
>
> When breaking down an objective into sub-objectives, it may be tempting to force the PCs to complete them one after another in a linear fashion. Not only can this remove some of the players’ agency, it’s not always how the real world works. The GM is essentially telling the players what they have to do, which ultimately feels boring.
>
> Instead, the players should be able to pursue different sub-objectives in the order of their choosing, resulting in a more freeform style of play. In such cases, the GM asks the players what they want to achieve next, and then the group frames a scene that showcases them pursuing that sub-objective and attempting to overcome an appropriate hurdle.

##### Erecting Hurdles

##### Martial Hurdles

> **SUPERNATURAL INVESTIGATION**
>
> Evidence gained from spirits is inadmissible in most courts, especially those that follow a strict reading of Imperial law.
>
> Further, the vagaries of spiritual insight make many lords skeptical of it—after all, the cryptic words of the kami have famously been misinterpreted on several occasions.
>
> The PCs can use it to establish facts for themselves, but will need to find stronger evidence (or, ideally, testimony) to prove points they wish to formally make.

> **VIGILANCE TO NOTICE**
>
> If you want to know if the PCs notice something without a roll to investigate the area, saying that all characters with vigilance of a certain value or higher notice a clue is one easy route to use. Just remember the vigilance values of your characters (which you can record on the GM’s sheet) before asking, lest they all miss something important!

- Which NPC stat blocks will you use, and which will you need to create from scratch?
- What are some ways players can spend (op) on their Initiative checks, and what sensory details can you provide to help evoke these possibilities in the players?
- What environmental or emotional factors may cause complications during the confrontation?

##### Social Hurdles

- Which NPC stat blocks will you use, and which will you need to create from scratch?
- How do the PCs compare to the NPCs in terms of honor, glory, and status? What does this mean for how the NPCs treat the PCs?
- What advantages and disadvantages do the NPCs possess that players can discover and use?
- What are some ways players can spend (op) on their checks, and what details can you provide to help evoke these possibilities in the players?
- Are there any special voices or mannerisms you can adopt to make the NPCs distinctive? Provide one memorable, unique detail for each.

##### Investigative Hurdles

- What information is absolutely necessary for the characters to progress to the next step?

Any story-stopping hurdles should be accessible to the players without a roll.

- What details might the PCs notice right away with various vigilance scores?
- What bonus information is accessible in the scene that isn’t necessary to advance to the next hurdle? What additional context or implications does it provide? What skills can be used to unlock those extra clues?
- Are there any other entities who are following the same trail, and what will they do if they discover the PCs are also interested?
- Are the PCs collecting the information in a socially or legally permissible manner? Remember that the testimony of an honorable samurai trumps evidence in the Rokugani justice system.
- What political ramifications does the unearthing of buried information have on social harmony? How will different factions react?

##### Logistical Hurdles

- Are there other items that might suit the needs? Sometimes, the best-suited items are also the hardest to acquire, and vice versa.
- Who is the current owner of the thing, and how important is it to them? Can the PCs pay for it or requisition it on behalf of their lord?
- Is there any history surrounding the thing itself (competing claims of ownership, curses, etc.) that complicates its acquisition?
- Is it illegal or socially frowned upon for the PCs to acquire the item? If so, how much honor will players need to stake to acquire the thing?
- Does the item need rules, and can you use existing rules for it or invent new ones?

##### Explorative Hurdles

##### Emotional Hurdles

- What are some situations that might force the problematic emotion or disadvantage to become apparent?
- What types of NPCs or personalities will exacerbate (or soothe) the problematic emotion? How can they be worked into the situation?
- How will NPCs and society react to the character when they express this emotional hurdle?
- What does the character need to do in order to counteract the effects of the problematic emotion or disadvantage? Is there a way that the character can unmask to temporarily overcome the hurdle, and what are the consequences?
- If this were a movie, what setting backdrop or props might the director choose to further illustrate the emotions in the scene?

##### Raising the Stakes

- Physical risks include injury, disease, and death, as well as loss of wealth or property. On the other hand, while Rokugani society considers physical rewards less impressive or important than other motivators, “getting stuff” can still be a powerful reward. Weapons and armor, equipment, property, and wealth all can serve as a reward and counterpart to physical risks.
- Social risks include embarrassment and shame, loss of position or reputation, losing friends and gaining enemies, or at its worst, exile. Social rewards include glory, titles, allies, and successful romance. Social rewards may also come with physical rewards, as status and property are often interlinked in Rokugani culture.
- Mental and emotional risks can be presented in the game by gaining new disadvantages, strife, or—more narratively—a character’s unhappiness, frustration, or impaired mental faculties. Rewards can include gaining new advantages, training in skills or techniques, or—more narratively—happiness, pride, and other positive emotions.
- Finally, Rokugan is a profoundly spiritual world. Characters can acquire karma from their actions, earn the enmity or patronage of specific Fortunes or spirits of the land, and if they traffic with foul forces, they might be afflicted with the Shadowlands Taint. Most spiritual risks and rewards can be represented in the game through certain advantages and disadvantages.

#### Using Lords and Duty

##### One Lord for the Whole Group

##### Multiple Lords for the Group

##### Shared Goals

##### Shared Circumstances

> **Conflicting Duties**
>
> Whenever the GM runs a multi-lord campaign, there is some inherent conflict between the characters’ giri as each lord pursues their objectives. This can and should be embraced as part of the fun of the game. Drama arises when there is conflict—when characters struggle to choose between betraying the group to benefit their clan, or betraying the clan in favor of their newfound friends.
>
> As the players create characters, the group should decide whether they are comfortable with this sort of drama. If not, then the GM should ensure that the characters’ giri are compatible, but for campaigns with split loyalties, there are two ways to handle intrigue between PCs: open and closed.
>
> In the open manner, which is recommended for most groups, players have fun knowing things their characters don’t, and pushing their characters into uncomfortable positions and revelations. It can help to have out-of-character discussion about how that story unfolds just to make sure everyone is on the same page. This mode also encourages more collaborative storytelling between the players and GM.
>
> In the closed manner, which is recommended only for experienced groups, players may pass notes to the GM or send messages in between sessions to describe what their character is up to. Part of the game becomes trying to unravel the other players’ secrets. Trust is key for this mode, and it’s important to discuss boundaries and expectations beforehand, such as whether it’s okay for characters to try to kill, steal from, or disgrace other characters.

> **ONE LORD, MYRIAD SAMURAI**
>
> Despite the need for secrecy, it isn’t unheard of for a lord to have members of other families from the same clan in their service if those samurai’s area of expertise is outside the purview of the lord’s family’s specialty. Because samurai from the same clan have pledged fealty to the clan champion, they are honor bound to work together to further the interests of the clan.

##### Creating a Lord

##### Ministers and Governors

> **Clan offices and ministers**
>
> - Advisor: A catch-all position with duties including general counsel and strategy.
> - Captain of the Guard: Oversees security forces of an area, assigns bodyguards.
> - Chancellor: The lord’s chief courtier, responsible for receiving dignitaries.
> - Court Astrologer: Responsible for auguries and divinatory rites.
> - General: Oversees the lord’s military forces, recruitment, and equipment.
> - Hatamoto: A daimyō’s personal retainer.
> - Herald: Oversees the lord’s communications with other samurai and clans.
> - Magistrate: Oversees law enforcement and the judiciary in a town, city, or province. May have deputies called yoriki.
> - Seneschal or Steward: Oversees the household staff and castle upkeep.
> - Spiritual Advisor: A font of knowledge related to local spirits or the Tao.
> - Stable Master: Oversees the lord’s horses, stablehands, and stables.

##### Provincial and Family Daimyō

- Concerns: Collecting taxes from citizenry and governors (or subordinate daimyō, for family daimyō/clan champions), ensuring security, cultivating and developing the land, levying armies, overseeing the sensei of the clan schools (family daimyō/clan champion), cultivating heirs, and approving interfamily and interclan marriage (family daimyō/clan champion).
- Complications: Environmental issues and disease, interests of neighboring provincial lords, peasant revolts, competing interests of neighboring families and clans, incompetent subordinates, meddling family members, Imperial ordinances.
- Retinue and Servants: Most player characters who attain the rank of samurai are eligible to serve as members of the provincial daimyō’s court. Provincial daimyō can also oversee city governors, clan magistrates, and ministers.

> **BUILDING TRUST**
>
> The game works best if neither the GM nor the players attempt to create “gotcha” moments, or use the rules to entrap the other party in a contradiction. It is fundamentally not a competition between the GM and the players (if for no other reason than that the GM wields final authority over the rules), and it operates most harmoniously when the rules are used to collaborate and tell a good story that everyone enjoys!

### Running the Game

#### Rules Adjudication

#### Multiple elemental Approach Options

##### Varying Difficulty in Text

##### Different Solutions, Different Challenges

> **SPURRING ADVANTAGE USE**
>
> If you notice your players aren’t making full use of their advantages, after a player fails a roll, ask them if they have any advantages that might apply. If they can’t think of one, see if one of the other players has an idea. If nobody can think of anything, move on—the idea is to remind them that advantages exist and encourage them to be creative in their use.

> **DESCRIBING ADVANTAGES AND DISADVANTAGES**
>
> When a player applies an advantage or disadvantage to a check, they should describe how it factors into the scene. Does the bushi’s rippling physique help them to cast aside the heavy stone in the group’s path? Does a courtier’s passion for tea mean that the scent of an exquisite cup dispels their worries after a stinging exchange with a rival? Filling in these details can help bring the story to life and reinforce that the characters are more than the numerical values of their rings and skills.

#### When to Reveal or Conceal the TN

For the majority of checks, we recommend the GM tell the players the TN. However, there are some rare occasions when the difficulty of a task would not be something the characters could accurately assess. Hidden TNs emphasize the mystery of a check, and can thus be good for showing that the PCs are out of their depth, such as when dealing with very powerful foes, supernatural beings, or unforeseen circumstances To compensate for the loss of available information, after a character performs a check with a hidden TN, the character gains 1 Void point.

#### Using Bonus Successes for Extra Effect

Sometimes, a check might yield specific additional results for bonus successes. For instance, many Attack action checks deal more damage if the character has bonus successes. The GM can put this to use on any check, allowing a character to achieve the goal more thoroughly with bonus successes.

