##### Example Title: Emerald Magistrate

Voice of Authority (Title Ability): Once per game session when making a Scheme or Support action check using a Social skill, you may choose additional targets equal to your Glory rank. You cannot choose any additional target with vigilance higher than the highest among your initial targets.

> **Peasant Family**
>
> Ring Increase: +1 to one ring of your choice Skill Increase: +1 to any two Trade skills of your choice Glory: 25 Starting Wealth: 10 zeni

> **SAMURAI AS FOES**
>
> In this playstyle, samurai are potentially one of the largest dangers to the PCs, They might be allies during a given story, for many samurai have use of characters like the PCs. However, most samurai will be unlikely to risk their lives for the PCs, and often view them as disposable.

### Alternative Campaign Styles

#### Rise from the Dust

##### Assumptions of this Playstyle

##### Character Creation Alterations

> **KEIJI (SAMPLE NEMURANAI WEAPON)**
>
> After being used to bring justice to countless mahō-tsukai, Shiba Seinaru’s blade awakened to its purpose of cleansing evil and bringing light to the darkness. At the end of a crisis, it vanished—along with its owner.
>
> Keiji is a katana with the The Fires from Within invocation.
>
> Quirk: Tasked to slay mahō-tsukai, the blade ignites and flickers with orange flames whenever the evil magic of these wicked sorcerers stirs nearby.

> **HARUKAZE (SAMPLE NEMURANAI ARMOR)**
>
> Said to have been one of the wonders woven by the legendary Asahina Setsuna and given to a lover in hostile lands so they might visit when they pleased, this item lay forgotten in a Lion Clan storehouse for generations before being rediscovered.
>
> Harukaze is a set of traveling clothes with the Cloak of Night invocation.
>
> Quirk: The pattern on Harukaze shifts with its surroundings, subtly mirroring the fashions and colors of the clothes around it.

#### Fate of the Realm

##### Assumptions of this Playstyle

##### Character Creation Alterations

Question 5: What is your character’s appointed quest? Instead of a giri, each PC has a quest: a task given them by their lord, by their own will, by supernatural forces, or perhaps by destiny itself. This task might be to set something right in the universe, recover or destroy a magical artifact, seal a particular evil entity again for another ten thousand years, or face a particular foe who threatens the entire realm. As with a giri, a character’s quest should be able to conflict with their ninjō, and sets the means by which the character will strive for glory. At the discretion of the group and the GM, all characters might share a quest, or they might have different quests with overlapping objectives.

Extra Experience: After character creation, each character receives 30 XP to spend on advancements.

##### Nemuranai

##### Awakened Weapon

A weapon nemuranai has the standard profile, plus:

- Its deadliness is increased by +2, and it has the Durable and Sacred qualities.
- It has a distinguishing quirk with narrative effects.
- It has one Attack or Scheme action invocation of Rank 1–3 sealed within it. Once per game session as an action, the wielder may perform this invocation, substituting the skill used to wield the weapon for Theology.

##### Awakened Armor

An armor nemuranai has the standard profile, plus:

- It has physical and supernatural resistance +2, and it has the Durable and Sacred qualities.
- It has a distinguishing quirk with narrative effects.
- It has one Support or Movement action invocation of Rank 1–3 sealed within it. Once per game session as an action, the wearer may perform this invocation, substituting Fitness for Theology.

##### Awakened Item

An item nemuranai has the standard profile, plus:

- It has the Durable and Sacred qualities.
- It has a distinguishing quirk with narrative effects.
- It has one invocation of Rank 1–4 sealed within it. Once per game session as an action, the holder may perform this invocation, substituting Meditation (or other skill of the GM’s choice) for Theology. While wielding this item, the character adds a kept (ring) set to an (op) result to their Theology checks using sealed invocation’s Element.

## CHAPTER 8

## Non-Player Characters

> **NPCs and Checks**
>
> GMs should keep the following advice in mind when rolling dice for NPCs.
>
> Rolling Dice: Keep the resolution of NPC rolls quick, using a bit of narration as needed to help the players understand what is going on.
>
> Spending (op): Don’t overthink it. (op) can inspire memorable moments, but not every check needs a cinematic turnabout. If it’s taking more than a few seconds, take a look at the NPC’s profile, which often contains abilities for (op)—or just let it go unspent.

> **NPCS ARE PEOPLE TOO**
>
> Remember that NPCs are more than just a set of rules and statistics! They are supporting characters, and can have interests, quirks, and desires on a narrative level beyond the statistics detailed in their profile. Such flourishes will help make them feel realized and compelling.

> **TRACKING NPC STRIFE**
>
> NPCs accumulate strife in the normal manner (see page 29), but the GM need only track their strife if it is actually likely to become important. Otherwise, strife they generate can simply be used as a general indicator for how to portray the character. If an NPC’s strife is being tracked and exceeds their composure, they should generally unmask at the start of their next turn unless they are an exceptionally disciplined individual.
>
> For guidance on dealing with strife with large groups of NPCs, see NPC Squads on page 317.

### NPC Profile Breakdown

- NPC Type: This denotes the importance of the NPC in the story.

Minions are minor characters, who have simplified profiles for ease of use.

Adversaries are narratively weighty characters, who have rules similar to a PC.

- Conflict Rank: Each NPC has a rank in two categories (combat (combat) and intrigue (intrigue)). These are estimates of the NPC’s capabilities in each of those spheres (See page 310 for details).
- Rings: Each NPC has a set of ring values (see The Five Rings, on page 32).
- Attributes: Each NPC has a set of attributes, just as a player character does (see Derived Attributes, on page 36).
- Demeanor: This section offers a simplified snapshot of an NPC’s personality, providing their responses to different approaches to Social skills (See page 310 for details).
- Advantages and Disadvantages: NPCs can be given prewritten advantages and disadvantages (see page 99), but printed NPC profiles usually have specialized advantages and disadvantages that do not appear in that chapter. These are treated as distinctions (or adversities) that apply as the GM deems appropriate.
- Skill Ranks: Each NPC has ranks in skill groups or skills that apply to their checks (see Chapter 3: Skills, on page 140).
- Favored Weapons and Gear: Any items the NPC is likely to have on their person, potentially including weapons they are likely to wield. Most own other possessions, but this section covers those most pertinent to their interactions with the PCs.
- Abilities: NPCs possess abilities, which sometimes include techniques also available to player characters and at other times consist of wholly new powers and effects. An NPC’s techniques sometimes differ from the versions available to player characters.

