#### Adding NPC Templates

##### Artist

Skills: +2 Artisan and Social skills

Advantages (add/replace 0–2): Eye for Detail (Air) [Artisan; Mental], Renown (Fire) [Artisan; Interpersonal], Steady Hand (Earth) [Artisan; Physical]

Disadvantages (add/replace 0–2): Cantankerous (Water) [Social; Interpersonal], Naiveté (Fire) [Social; Interpersonal], World-Weariness (Earth) [Social; Interpersonal]

Demeanor (replace 1): Detached, Gruff, Shrewd

##### Investigator

Conflict Rank: Combat +2, Intrigue +2

Skills: +1 Martial and Scholar Skills

Advantages (add/replace 0–2): Analytical Mind (Fire) [Scholar; Mental], Self-Awareness (Void) [Martial; Mental], Sharp Eyes (Water) [Scholar; Physical]

Disadvantages (add/replace 0–2): Disbeliever (Fire) [Social; Mental], Haughty Tone (Air) [Social; Interpersonal], Severe Allergies (Earth) [Martial; Physical]

Techniques (add 0–2): Kata (kata), Shūji (shuji)

Demeanor (replace 1): Gruff, Shrewd

##### Sage

Conflict Rank: Intrigue +2, Combat +1

Skills: +1 Scholar and Trade skills

Advantages (add/replace 0–2): Forbidden Knowledge [Mahō] (Fire) [Scholar; Mental], Exact Memory (Earth) [Scholar; Mental], Well-Read (Fire) [Scholar; Mental]

Disadvantages (add/replace 0–2): Bitter Marriage (Water) [Social; Interpersonal], Know-It-All (Air) [Social; Interpersonal], Frailty (Earth) [Martial; Physical]

Techniques (add 0–2): Mahō ((maho)), Shūji (shuji)

Demeanor (replace 1): Detached, Shrewd

##### Socialite

Skills: +2 Social and Trade skills

Advantages (add/replace 0–2): Well-Spoken (Air) [Social; Interpersonal], Prim (Earth) [Social; Interpersonal], Seems Trustworthy (Water) [Social; Interpersonal]

Disadvantages (add/replace 0–2): Doomed Love (Fire) [Social; Interpersonal], Lack of Empathy (Air) [Social; Mental], Bad Eyesight (Water) [Scholar; Physical]

Demeanor (replace 1): Assertive, Shrewd

##### Spiritualist

Conflict Rank: Combat +1, Intrigue +1

Skills: +1 Artisan and Scholar Skills

Advantages (add/replace 0–2): Partial Enlightenment (Void) [Scholar; Spiritual], Keen Empathy (Air) [Social; Interpersonal], Friendly Kami or Spirit (Fire, Earth, Air, or Water) [Scholar; Spiritual]

Disadvantages (add/replace 0–2): Poverty (Water) [Trade; Material], Fanaticism (Fire) [Social; Mental], Repressed (Fire) [Social; Mental]

Techniques (add 0–2): Invocations (invocation), Kihō (kiho)

Demeanor (replace 1): Detached, Assertive

##### Trickster

Conflict Rank: Combat +1, Intrigue +2

Skills: +1 Social and Scholar skills

Advantages (add/replace 0–2): Excellent Liar (Air) [Social; Interpersonal], Patience (Earth) [Scholar; Mental], Loves Haggling (Water) [Trade; Interpersonal]

Disadvantages (add/replace 0–2): Fear of Failure (Fire) [Martial; Mental], Narcissism (Earth) [Social; Mental], Unmemorable Face (Air) [Social; Physical]

Techniques (add 0–2): Ninjutsu (ninjutsu), Shūji (shuji)

Demeanor (replace 1): Ambitious, Shrewd

##### Warrior

Conflict Rank: Combat +2, Intrigue +1

Skills: +2 Martial and Social skills

Advantages (add/replace 0–2): Masterful Fighter (Earth) [Martial; Mental], Fearsome Presence (Fire) [Social; Interpersonal], Veteran’s Instincts (Water) [Scholar; Mental]

Disadvantages (add/replace 0–2): Bad Dreams (Void) [Martial; Mental], Quick to Anger (Fire) [Social; Interpersonal], Shattered by War (Earth) [Social; Mental]

Demeanor (replace 1): Assertive, Gruff

> **Closed Versus Open Rolls**
>
> As the GM, it’s your choice whether you want to make a given roll for an NPC open (in sight of the players) or closed (behind a screen or otherwise obscured). It’s a good idea to make most rolls openly, because of abilities that allow PCs to alter enemy rolls, but this isn’t a hard-and-fast rule.
>
> If a player wishes to use an ability that alters an enemy roll, you can perform the alteration for them on the NPC’s dice pool (in an optimal manner for the player’s desired outcome). If for some reason even this would give away too much information, you can give that character 1 Void point (and they do not count as having used the ability), but this is an option you probably shouldn’t exercise too often.

> **NPC STATS**
>
> Note that NPCs can have ring values, derived attributes, and abilities that a PC cannot possess. These are set at specific values based on the intended function of the NPC, rather than generated with a formula as they would be for a player character.

### Sample NPCs

### Subjects of Rokugan

#### Loyal Bushi

Gear (equipped): Lacquered armor (Physical 4, Ceremonial, Cumbersome, Wargear), wakizashi, quiver, yari (spear), knife

Gear (other): Plain robes (Physical 1, Mundane), a handful of koku and bu

> **COMMONERS**
>
> There are two broad types of commoners in Rokugan: the heimin, or “half-persons,” who make up the caste of laborers known as the bonge and do much of the fundamental labor required to allow the Empire to function; and the hinin, or so-called “nonpeople,” who perform many of the most base and demeaning jobs.
>
> Within the category of heimin, there is further stratification. In order of social importance, the first are farmers, then artisans and craftspeople, and finally merchants, who make nothing themselves and only deal in trade and commerce.
>
> Hinin include criminals, many entertainers, and those considered “untouchables” because they handle dead flesh and refuse. Hinin are considered “nonpeople” and treated as little more than property.

> **PLAYER CHARACTERS AND SAMURAI NPCS**
>
> An NPC samurai who is out and about is likely conducting business for their lord. A samurai encountered on the road might be making a routine patrol, serving as an escort for important people or cargoes, or acting as a courier. They are generally busy, and give formal but brief greetings to other samurai.
>
> If they are encountered in another location, such as an inn, shrine, or temple, however, they may be more open to interactions with the player characters.

#### Humble Peasant

Gear (equipped): Rags, cloth headband, shoddy farm equipment, a meager handful of zeni

#### Seasoned Courtier

Gear (equipped): Extravagant robes (Physical 1, Ceremonial), calligraphy set

Gear (other): Tea set, Rokugani pony

> **PLAYER CHARACTERS AND PEASANT NPCS**
>
> Heimin should be politely deferential toward the PCs in most encounters. However, they are vassals of a clan, so making unreasonable demands or being wantonly abusive to them will likely rouse the ire of their lord.
>
> Ashigaru will defer to the PCs, but with a degree of practiced ease that most farmers lack—they are used to encountering and dealing with samurai.

#### Scholarly Shugenja

Gear (equipped): Sanctified robes (Physical 1, Supernatural 3, Ceremonial), scroll satchel, offerings

#### Trained Ashigaru

Gear (equipped): Ashigaru armor (Physical 3, Wargear), knife, dice and cup or musical instrument, a handful of bu and zeni

#### Venerable Provincial Daimyō

Gear (equipped): Calligraphy set, personal chop

Gear (other): Daishō, plated armor (Physical 5, Cumbersome, Durable, Wargear), various estates

##### Outlaws and Rōnin

#### Desperate Bandit

Gear (equipped): Thick clothes (Physical 2, Mundane), knife, pouch of slingstones, drinking cup

> **Twists of Fate**
>
> As the GM, there are times when it is not narratively satisfying for a villain to just die. To avoid this, you can apply a twist of fate—an unpreventable outcome that saves the NPC, but also compensates the players. First, tell the players about the twist of fate and narrate how the NPC escapes. At the end of the session, each PC receives 1 extra XP, as they come to terms with fortune's turns and grow stronger in anticipation of their eventual clash with their foe.

> **MUSHA SHUGYŌ**
>
> With the permission of their daimyō, a clan samurai could undertake a musha shugyō, or “warrior’s pilgrimage.” A samurai on a musha shugyō becomes a rōnin, temporarily casting aside their loyalties and wandering the Empire to learn as much as possible while perfecting their skills as a warrior. It is essential that the erstwhile samurai make no reference to their clan during the pilgrimage. Once the requirements of the musha shugyō are fulfilled, the rōnin returns to their clan as a samurai.

#### Experienced Bandit

Gear (equipped): Ashigaru armor (Physical 3, Wargear), assortment of knives, sake jug, handful of bu

#### Skillful Rōnin

Gear (equipped): Wakizashi, worn traveler's robes (Physical 2, Mundane), quiver, empty sake jug, straw hat, handful of bu and zeni

| DIRTY TRICKS | STRIKING AS FIRE |
|---|---|
| When performing an Attack action check, an experienced bandit may spend (op) in the following way: | When performing an Attack action (Fire) check, a rōnin may spend (op) as follows: |
| (op): One character at range 0–2 suffers the Disoriented condition. Then you may move 1 range band. | Fire (op)+: Increase the severity of any critical strike dealt as part of this attack by 2 per (op) spent this way. |
| ...GET THEM, YOU FOOLS! |  |

> **NPC Squads**
>
> Having a large number of NPCs in a conflict can make it chaotic for any GM, especially if each one has an initiative value. To streamline handling a group of NPCs of the same type, treat them as a squad. A squad consists of two or more NPCs with the same name and profile, and functions as follows:
>
> Initiative: They generate a single initiative value, and take turns in any order you choose at their initiative value each round.
>
> Group Actions: If you want to reduce the number of rolls the squad makes, simply have all members of the squad but one take the Assist action targeting the one member who performs a different action. Having more than six NPCs in a squad is not advised, simply due to the size of the dice pools involved.
>
> Group Stances: All members of a squad should assume the same stance each round.
>
> Group Strife: Assign the squad a squad strife value equal to 3 times an individual member’s composure. This can be helpful as a means to determine the point at which undisciplined foes likely break and run.
>
> Generally, you’ll want to make squads of minion NPCs like ashigaru and bandits, since they’ll be the bulk of unnamed participants in a skirmish, but there’s no mechanical reason you couldn’t have a squad of adversaries—they’d just be more complex to manage and likely more dangerous to the PCs.

> **PLAYER CHARACTERS AND CULTIST NPCS**
>
> Because they are rejected by mainstream Rokugani society, and most are considered to be in violation of Imperial laws dealing with blasphemy, cults of any type tend to be secretive in the extreme. Clandestine cultists can operate at all levels of society, however, and might be the foes of the PCs.

### Cultists

#### Deadly Bloodspeaker

Gear (equipped): Sorcerous raiment (Physical 2, Supernatural 4, Unholy), set of vile scrolls

> **MOON CULTISTS**
>
> Onnotangu, or Lord Moon, figures prominently in the prehistory of Rokugan. Because of his wrath, fear, and jealousy, the Kami were cast out of Heaven and fell into Ningen-dō at the dawn of the Empire. Twelve centuries later, there are still those in the Empire who consider Lord Moon to be the preeminent divine figure, devoting themselves to him in clandestine ceremonies.
>
> For the Moon Cultist profile, use the Wicked Mahō-tsukai but replace their mahō with these invocations (invocation): By the Light of the Lord Moon, Summon Fog, Tempest of Air, and Vapor of Nightmares.

> **OTHERWORLDLY AND TAINTED BEINGS**
>
> Some beings are described as Otherworldly (ethereal beings beyond the ken of humans), Tainted (beings under the sway of Fu Leng), or both.
>
> These classifications do not have any intrinsic rules, but many abilities and some item qualities refer to them.

#### Wicked Mahō-Tsukai

Gear (equipped): Sorcerous raiment (Physical 2, Supernatural 4, Unholy), set of vile scrolls, concealing mask or cowl, several bu

#### Shadowlands Horrors

#### Bushi Skeleton

Gear (equipped): Ancient armor (Physical 3, Wargear)

> **BOG HAGS IN THE EMPIRE**
>
> Because bog hags are difficult to detect, groups such as Bloodspeaker cells may make deals with them, using them to infiltrate Rokugani society in return for providing an ongoing supply of victims. This can add another, somewhat paranoid, dimension to a campaign, as player characters could be prompted to suspect nearly anyone of being a bog hag, either with its own motives or working on behalf of sinister powers.

#### Bog Hag

Gear (equipped): Fresh skin (Physical 3, Spiritual 5, Unholy), 3 doses of each poison (see page 244), human-skin scrolls of wicked lore

> **CREATING THE UNDEAD**
>
> If a mortal body is left near a place permeated by the Taint of Jigoku—for instance, in the Shadowlands—or in a corrupted location such as parts of the Shinomen Mori or the ruins of the Castle of the Snake in the northern Empire, it may spontaneously reanimate as a result of possession by a kansen, an evil elemental spirit. Such undead are mindlessly destructive.
>
> Undead may also be created through the deliberate efforts of mahō-tsukai, either by means of powerful rituals or through the use of porcelain masks imbued with evil power and placed on the bodies to be reanimated. These masks give a mahō-tsukai a degree of control over the undead being.

#### Shadowlands Goblin

Gear (equipped): Scavenged ashigaru armor (Physical 3), quiver, teeth or other grotesque trophies

#### Sinister Oni

Gear (equipped): Demonic hide and armor scraps (Physical 5, Spiritual 5, Unholy)

#### Zombie Peasant

Gear (equipped): Decaying flesh (Physical 2)

### Spirits and strange beings

#### Forest Troll

Gear (equipped): Scaled hide (Physical 1, Supernatural 1), animal-pelt clothes, polished skull collection

> **GHOSTS**
>
> Most of the time, PCs can’t fight ghosts with force of arms—instead, ghosts often work best as narrative obstacles. The PCs might need to determine the cause of their unrest or their agenda and then help to resolve it or purify the spirit.
>
> If a ghost needs to make a check, they can use their profile in life, with the following alterations:
>
> They are an Otherworldly being.
>
> They have Physical Resistance 10.
>
> They are incorporeal, and can perform ghostly phenomena. At the GM’s discretion, they have 1 or more Air invocations to represent their spiritual powers.

#### Ki-Rin

Gear (equipped): Magic hide (Physical 3, Supernatural 3)

#### Manifest Air Kami

Gear (equipped): Misty Mantle (Physical 5, Supernatural 2)

#### Manifest Earth Kami

Gear (equipped): Rocky Hide (Physical 6, Sacred)

#### Manifest Fire Kami

Gear (equipped): Corona of Flames (Physical 3)

#### Manifest Water Kami

Gear (equipped): Enshrouding Fog (Physical 3, Supernatural 4)

> **ANIMAL CHECKS**
>
> Animals don't generally have ranks in Trade skills, but if they need to roll it, any wild animal has Survival rank 2 and any domesticated animal has Survival rank 1.

### Animals

#### Bear

Gear (equipped): Thick hide (Physical 4)

#### Boar

Gear (equipped): Thick hide (Physical 4)

#### Cat

> **Mounts and Riding**
>
> While it is being ridden during a conflict, a mount does not take a turn of its own, and provides the following benefits to its rider:
>
> - When the rider succeeds on a Movement action check, add bonus successes equal to the mount's Water Ring.
> - The rider may use their Survival skill in place of their Fitness skill for any Fitness check they make.
>
> During narrative and downtime scenes, a mounted character doubles their overland travel speed.

> **LARGE AND SMALL DOGS**
>
> Dogs come in a variety of sizes, from small hounds for rabbit hunting to massive beasts bred for battle. These can be represented with through one of the following sets of modifications to the Dog profile:
>
> Large dog: silhouette 2. physical resistance +1, endurance +2, conflict rank (combat) +1
>
> Small dog: silhouette 1. vigilance +1

#### Horse (Rokugani Pony)

Gear (equipped): Hide (Physical 2), saddle, saddlebags, feed bag, grooming kit

> **UNICORN WARHORSES**
>
> Unicorn warhorses are vastly superior to Rokugani ponies as warhorses. A Unicorn warhorse can be represented with the following modifications to the Rokugani Pony profile:
>
> Remove the Skittish disadvantage, add one advantage from the Warrior template, endurance +4, composure +4, conflict rank (combat) +1

#### Hunting Cat

Gear (equipped): Thick fur (Physical 2)

#### Utaku Steed

Gear (equipped): Hide and armor (Physical 4), saddle, saddlebags, feed bag, grooming kit

#### Wolf

Gear (equipped): Hide (Physical 2)

